360 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 319 0 R
/T (Engineering colleges in India vary considerably in placement performance, employability outcomes, internship participation and average starting salary. Recruiters, placement cells, students and institutional management often lack a consolidated, data-driven view that connects these outcomes to branch, tier, ownership, category, state and skill-related factors. This project addresses that gap by building an analytical dataset of 22,386 records across 2,813 unique colleges, 33 states, 7 engineering branches, 26 categories, 7 ownership types and 4 tiers, and by developing a Power BI decision-support framework on top of it. )
/E (Engineering colleges in India vary considerably in placement performance, employability outcomes, internship participation and average starting salary. Recruiters, placement cells, students and institutional management often lack a consolidated, data-driven view that connects these outcomes to branch, tier, ownership, category, state and skill-related factors. This project addresses that gap by building an analytical dataset of 22,386 records across 2,813 unique colleges, 33 states, 7 engineering branches, 26 categories, 7 ownership types and 4 tiers, and by developing a Power BI decision-support framework on top of it. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
361 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 319 0 R
/T (The central business problem examined is why engineering college placements are low in aggregate, which institutional and branch-level factors are associated with stronger placement outcomes, and how recruiters can identify colleges and branches that demonstrate stronger placement, employability, internship participation, salary outcomes and recruiter readiness. )
/E (The central business problem examined is why engineering college placements are low in aggregate, which institutional and branch-level factors are associated with stronger placement outcomes, and how recruiters can identify colleges and branches that demonstrate stronger placement, employability, internship participation, salary outcomes and recruiter readiness. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
362 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 319 0 R
/T (The dataset was cleaned and standardized through a structured methodology covering column identification, data-type validation, missing-value inspection, duplicate inspection, state standardization, category and ownership cleaning, branch standardization, numerical validation and analytical grouping, using College_Name, State_Clean and Branch_Name as the analytical uniqueness key. The final dataset contains zero missing values and zero duplicate analytical records. )
/E (The dataset was cleaned and standardized through a structured methodology covering column identification, data-type validation, missing-value inspection, duplicate inspection, state standardization, category and ownership cleaning, branch standardization, numerical validation and analytical grouping, using College_Name, State_Clean and Branch_Name as the analytical uniqueness key. The final dataset contains zero missing values and zero duplicate analytical records. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
363 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 319 0 R
/T (Descriptive statistics show an overall mean placement percentage of approximately 63.13%, mean average salary of approximately 5.78 LPA, mean internship participation of approximately 54.69%, and mean employability indicator of approximately 61.72%. Branch-level analysis indicates that Computer Science Engineering records the highest average placement \(74.36%\) and salary \(7.16 LPA\), while Civil Engineering records the lowest average placement \(49.34%\) and salary \(4.28 LPA\). Tier-level analysis indicates that Tier 1 institutions show the strongest average performance across all four quantitative indicators, while Tier 3 and Tier 4 show relatively similar average performance. )
/E (Descriptive statistics show an overall mean placement percentage of approximately 63.13%, mean average salary of approximately 5.78 LPA, mean internship participation of approximately 54.69%, and mean employability indicator of approximately 61.72%. Branch-level analysis indicates that Computer Science Engineering records the highest average placement \(74.36%\) and salary \(7.16 LPA\), while Civil Engineering records the lowest average placement \(49.34%\) and salary \(4.28 LPA\). Tier-level analysis indicates that Tier 1 institutions show the strongest average performance across all four quantitative indicators, while Tier 3 and Tier 4 show relatively similar average performance. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
364 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 319 0 R
/T (A project-defined Recruiter Readiness framework was developed using Placement Percentage, Employability Indicator and Internship Participation to classify institutions as Recruiter Ready, Moderately Ready or Needs Improvement. This framework, together with descriptive fields on curriculum subjects, recruiter demand, industry skills, and skill gaps, was translated into a set of Power BI dashboards supporting recruiter-oriented college and branch shortlisting. )
/E (A project-defined Recruiter Readiness framework was developed using Placement Percentage, Employability Indicator and Internship Participation to classify institutions as Recruiter Ready, Moderately Ready or Needs Improvement. This framework, together with descriptive fields on curriculum subjects, recruiter demand, industry skills, and skill gaps, was translated into a set of Power BI dashboards supporting recruiter-oriented college and branch shortlisting. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
